  VEGCODE `Nr. of candidates` `Highest membership`
  &lt;chr&gt;                 &lt;int&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;
1 AWU                       6                    1
2 NAD                       6                    1
3 RKX                       6                    1
4 SWR                       6                    1


To inspect how individual interpreter profiles evolved between Round 1 and Round 2, we use ecoSynthesis::membership_tile_plot() side by side via cowplot::plot_grid().






WarningPackage caveat: ecoSynthesis::membership_tile_plot() in v0.0.0.9005



Two minor bugs affect membership_tile_plot() in ecoSynthesis v0.0.0.9005: 1. Passing round = 2 inside membership_tile_plot(data, round = 2) triggers a column-masking bug (dplyr::filter(round == round)), which retains all rounds instead of filtering. Always pre-filter data by round before calling membership_tile_plot(). 2. The plot title is currently hardcoded to "Round 1 Cross-Referencing", so for Round 2 plots we explicitly override it with + ggplot2::labs(title = "Round 2 Cross-Referencing").


Looking at SWR (Western regrowth complex) reveals a persistent case of divergent opinions where interpreters still favour different target EFGs after group discussion:

r1plot &lt;- TASVEG_clean |&gt;
    filter(VEGCODE == "SWR", round == 1) |&gt;
    membership_tile_plot() +
    labs(title = "Round 1 Cross-Referencing")

r2plot &lt;- TASVEG_clean |&gt;
    filter(VEGCODE == "SWR", round == 2) |&gt;
    membership_tile_plot() +
    labs(title = "Round 2 Cross-Referencing")

plot_grid(
    r1plot,
    r2plot,
    ncol = 2,
    labels = c("A", "B"),
    align = "hv"
)








In contrast, RKX (Highland rainforest scrub) illustrates convergent opinions, where group discussion helped interpreters align more closely around shared primary candidates in Round 2:

r1plot &lt;- TASVEG_clean |&gt;
    filter(VEGCODE == "RKX", round == 1) |&gt;
    membership_tile_plot() +
    labs(title = "Round 1 Cross-Referencing")

r2plot &lt;- TASVEG_clean |&gt;
    filter(VEGCODE == "RKX", round == 2) |&gt;
    membership_tile_plot() +
    labs(title = "Round 2 Cross-Referencing")

plot_grid(
    r1plot,
    r2plot,
    ncol = 2,
    labels = c("A", "B"),
    align = "hv"
)

7.3 Comparing broad TASVEG vegetation groups
Source classifications often group communities by physiognomy or floristics rather than the functional criteria of IUCN GET (Keith et al. 2022). Comparing membership summaries within broad VEG_GROUP classes highlights where source and target hierarchies align versus where a single TASVEG group spans multiple IUCN GET functional groups.
First, we list the communities in the Native grassland group:

TASVEG |&gt;
    filter(VEG_GROUP == "Native grassland") |&gt;
    select(VEG_GROUP, VEGCODE, VEGCODE_D) |&gt;
    DT::datatable()





Joining TASVEG_clean with TASVEG and summarising by EFG shows that Native grassland units split cleanly across multiple target EFGs (such as temperate grasslands vs subalpine/alpine or wetland types), a dichotomy recognised consistently across interpreters:

TASVEG_clean |&gt;
    left_join(TASVEG, by = "VEGCODE") |&gt;
    filter(VEG_GROUP == "Native grassland") |&gt;
    group_by(EFG) |&gt;
    summarise(
        `Nr. VEGCODE` = n_distinct(VEGCODE),
        `Nr. experts` = n_distinct(expert),
        `Mean memberships` = mean(memb)
    ) |&gt;
    arrange(desc(`Nr. experts`), desc(`Nr. VEGCODE`))

